//! Cosine table for the 32-point DCT-II used by the perceptual hash.
//!
//! **Generated. Do not edit.** Source of truth is
//! `gpu/testdata/dct-cos-8x32.json`, which `gpu/js/parity.mjs` loads directly;
//! this file is derived from it so the Rust side needs no JSON parser in the
//! library. A test asserts the two still agree.
//!
//! Why a table at all, rather than calling `cos`:
//!
//! V8 does not use the platform libm for `Math.cos` — it bundles its own
//! fdlibm port. So JavaScript and Rust cosines differ in the last bit, and
//! the median threshold in a perceptual hash amplifies that into flipped
//! bits. Measured: the two implementations disagreed on 3 of 64 bits for a
//! 32-pixel checker pattern, which is how this was found.
//!
//! With the table shared the DCT becomes pure multiply-add, which *is*
//! bit-identical in IEEE-754 for a fixed operation order — and both sides
//! document that order.
//!
//! Regenerate:
//! ```sh
//! cargo test -p lwdbg-gpu --features gpu --test golden_vectors \\
//!     emit_dct_table -- --ignored --nocapture
//! ```

// clippy::excessive_precision is correct that some of these literals carry
// more digits than the value needs — 1.00000000000000000e0 is 1.0. It is
// still wrong for this file.
//
// Every literal is emitted at exactly 17 significant digits because that is
// what round-trips an f64 exactly, and because gpu/testdata/dct-cos-8x32.json
// holds the same text for the JavaScript side. Truncating here would make the
// two files textually different, and would invite exactly the kind of tidying
// that breaks the bit-identity this table exists to guarantee.
#![allow(clippy::excessive_precision)]

